request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,5792,27853,32507,291,2248,374,264,46797,448,3040,12300,11,1817,315,892,374,458,3232,42939,21495,382,10061,400,1867,6484,3,387,264,5792,27853,32507,291,2248,323,1077,400,47,3,387,279,4911,1459,3232,307,11202,504,3501,400,32,8161,11289,27266,12947,70083,57960,1975,19491,90,2537,31716,311,4201,3579,400,4897,35,3,518,1459,400,48,12947,3555,374,279,11341,400,47,48,10360,48,3,937,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA regular tetrahedron is a pyramid with four faces, each of which is an equilateral triangle.\n\nLet $ABCD$ be a regular tetrahedron and let $P$ be the unique point equidistant from points $A,B,C,D$. Extend $\\overrightarrow{AP}$ to hit face $BCD$ at point $Q$. What is the ratio $PQ/AQ$ ?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"79500257828157cbaa3a7c4a2028cb8b7ed769aca498dd4f36309942a8d93284"},"seconds":15.018197059631348,"seed":774072640888213963,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to determine the ratio \\( \\frac{PQ}{AQ} \\) where \\( P \\) is the centroid of the regular tetrahedron \\(ABCD\\) and \\(Q\\) is the point where the line \\(AP\\) intersects the face \\(BCD\\).\n\nFirst, let's establish some properties of the regular tetrahedron. The centroid \\(P\\) of a regular tetrahedron divides each median in the ratio 3:1, with the longer segment being from the vertex to the centroid. This means that if we consider the median from vertex \\(A\\) to the centroid of the opposite face \\(BCD\\), the centroid \\(P\\) is located such that \\(AP:PP' = 3:1\\), where \\(P'\\) is the centroid of the face \\(BCD\\).\n\nThe centroid \\(P'\\) of the face \\(BCD\\) is the point where the medians of the equilateral triangle \\(BCD\\) intersect. Since \\(P'\\) is the centroid of the equilateral triangle, it divides each median in the ratio 2:1. Therefore, the distance from \\(P'\\) to any vertex of the triangle (say \\(B\\)) is \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\) of the median length of the triangle.\n\nNow, consider the line segment \\(AP\\) which extends to intersect the face \\(BCD\\) at point \\(Q\\). Since \\(P\\) is the centroid of the tetrahedron, it is located at a distance of \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\) of the height of the tetrahedron from the base \\(BCD\\). The point \\(Q\\) is the intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with the plane of the face \\(BCD\\). Because \\(P\\) is the centroid, the line \\(AP\\) is a median of the tetrahedron, and it intersects the plane of the face \\(BCD\\) at a point that divides the segment \\(AP\\) in the ratio 3:1.\n\nTo find the ratio \\( \\frac{PQ}{AQ} \\), we note that \\(P\\) is located at \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\) of the height of the tetrahedron from the base \\(BCD\\), and \\(Q\\) is the point where the line \\(AP\\) intersects the plane \\(BCD\\). Since \\(P\\) is the centroid, the line \\(AP\\) is divided into segments \\(PQ\\) and \\(AQ\\) such that \\(PQ:QG = 1:3\\) where \\(G\\) is the centroid of the face \\(BCD\\). Therefore, the ratio \\( \\frac{PQ}{AQ} = \\frac{1}{3} \\).\n\nThus, the ratio \\( \\frac{PQ}{AQ} \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:166"}
